(Plan 327 Part B: the bytes this program will find on its stdin, and
 whether it was given any reads to consume them with. Written to the
 per-run scratch directory by the gen loop and handed to BOTH of the
 oracle's runs — the same rule as argv, for the same reason.

 Unlike argv, the generator does NOT assert on these. It knows what
 bytes it wrote, but a program's reaction to them runs through buffer
 sizing, truncation and EOF, and predicting the result would mean
 reimplementing those semantics in the generator — which is how a
 fuzzer ends up asserting its own bugs. The bytes are here to be
 CONSUMED, not predicted: the finding is a crash, a hang, or output
 that disagrees with itself between runs.)

a text called gen_input is "".

(Scratch accumulator for 'gen build input', program level and 'clear'ed
 on entry (Defect 17): a function-local declaration here is never freed
 until process exit, so a per-call buffer would still be one 4 KB
 allocation per generated program, unbounded over a campaign.)

a buffer called 'the built input' is "".

a boolean called gen_reads_stdin is false.

(Whether this program does real file I/O. The path comes in on the
 command line — the harness knows the program's per-run scratch
 directory and passes it as a flag, which is only possible now that argv
 exists at all. Confined by construction: the program can only write
 where it was told, that directory is per-run and pid-keyed, and the
 existing sweep removes it. No allowlist enforcement is needed because
 the program is never given another path to try.)

a boolean called gen_does_file_io is false.

(Counters, so a program that COMMITTED to an input channel is guaranteed
 to use it — the same guarantee the grid sink already has. Without it
 the leaf still has to win a 1-in-45 kind draw, so committing meant
 little: measured over 150 programs, stdin reads appeared in 9 and file
 I/O in 22, against a 50% commitment rate each. A channel worth
 deciding on is worth exercising.)

a number called gen_stdin_reads is 0.

a number called gen_file_ios is 0.

(File I/O, write half only — plan 324 Part B, partially unblocked.

 The plan puts this behind the per-run sandbox, but the WRITE half needs
 no sandbox at all: `/dev/stdout` is a path every process already has,
 writing to it cannot escape anywhere, and it never blocks.

 The READ half is deliberately NOT emitted, and the reason is recorded
 as defect 7 in docs/FUZZER_DEFECTS.md: `supervise` forks and Executes
 the generated program with no stdio redirection, so the child inherits
 the fuzzer's own stdin. A generated `/dev/stdin` read therefore blocks
 until the deadline and is classified `hang` — a false finding on every
 such program. Proved before emitting: with `< /dev/null` the read
 returns 0 bytes and exits 0; with an open-but-silent pipe it blocks
 until killed. The harness must redirect the child's stdin before the
 read half can be emitted.

 This leaf also reaches the FILE PROPERTY surface, which is where the
 red team found #37 (`readable` always true) and #38 (`exists` is a
 parse error) — the least-covered documented surface in the language.
 `exists` is not emitted because it does not compile; `readable` IS
 emitted even though #37 makes it always answer 1, because a wrong
 answer is not a crash and the program still runs. When #37 is fixed
 this leaf starts exercising the fix for free.)
(DISABLED 2026-08-20, and the reason is worth keeping.

 Writing to "/dev/stdout" from a generated program corrupts the
 HARNESS's own captured output. Opening /dev/stdout gives the child an
 independent file description with its own offset starting at 0. When
 the harness's stdout is a redirected FILE - which is exactly what
 test.sh does when capturing a golden - the child writes at offset 0
 while the parent goes on writing at its own, much larger offset. The
 result is a sparse file: a hole between them that reads back as a run
 of NUL bytes, which is how this was found (tests/060_loop_gen.expected
 went from text to binary, 526 NULs, passing locally on a terminal and
 failing in CI where stdout is a file).

 It is not a compiler bug - `Write` itself is clean, 6 bytes for 6
 bytes - and not visible interactively. It is a consequence of choosing
 /dev/stdout as the "needs no sandbox" write target, which was my
 shortcut around plan 324 Part B.

 The correct target is a path inside the per-run scratch directory,
 which defect 5's isolation now provides. That needs the harness to
 tell the program where its scratch dir is - the same plumbing plan 324
 T3 needs for seeded input, and the same plumbing defect 7 needs for
 stdin. The leaf is kept intact so it can be re-pointed at that path
 rather than rewritten.

 The leaf also printed `permissions`, which is machine-dependent for
 /dev/stdout - a tty, a pipe and a file all differ - so it could never
 have lived in a golden fixture either way.)

To 'gen leaf file write' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called n is gen_texts.
    a number called 'the payload' is 'rng below' of 100000.
    a text called 'open line' is "open a file for writing called fh{n} at \"/dev/stdout\"".
    a text called 'write line' is "Write \"fuzz{n} {'the payload'}\\n\" to fh{n}".
    a text called 'descriptor line' is "Print fh{n}'s descriptor".
    a text called 'writable line' is "Print fh{n}'s writable".
    a text called 'readable line' is "Print fh{n}'s readable".
    a text called 'permissions line' is "Print fh{n}'s permissions".
    a text called 'close line' is "Close fh{n}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['open line', 'write line', 'descriptor line', 'writable line', 'readable line', 'permissions line', 'close line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Reading stdin. Both documented spellings are emitted — a path
 ("/dev/stdin") and the descriptor (0) — because they are different code
 paths through the compiler, and LANGUAGE.md offers both.

 The buffer is deliberately sometimes SMALLER than the input the harness
 wrote, because truncation is where buffer bugs live. Verified against
 0.4.7 before emitting: a 4-byte buffer given 10 bytes reads 4, a second
 read on the same handle at EOF returns 0, and empty input reads 0 —
 none of which crash or raise.

 This leaf is only ever emitted when the harness will actually supply
 input, and it requires defect 7's fix: before that, the child inherited
 the fuzzer's stdin and any read here would have blocked until the
 deadline and been misreported as a hang.)

To 'gen leaf stdin read' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    (A program given no input still reads legally — it gets EOF — but
     emitting the read only when there is something to consume keeps the
     interesting case common rather than incidental.)
    If gen_reads_stdin is false then,
        'gen leaf print' of indent,
        Return a boolean, false.
    Set gen_stdin_reads to gen_stdin_reads add 1.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called n is gen_texts.
    a number called 'the size' is 'rng below' of 60 add 4.
    a number called 'by descriptor' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a text called 'the source' is "\"/dev/stdin\"".
    If 'by descriptor' is 1 then,
        Set 'the source' to "0".
    a text called 'open line' is "open a file for reading called sin{n} at {'the source'}".
    a text called 'buffer line' is "a buffer called sb{n} is {'the size'} bytes".
    a text called 'read line' is "Read from sin{n} into sb{n}".
    a text called 'size line' is "Print sb{n}'s size".
    a text called 'empty line' is "If sb{n} is empty then, Print \"sb{n} empty\". Otherwise, Print \"sb{n} filled\"".
    a text called 'second read' is "a buffer called sc{n} is 8 bytes".
    a text called 'second line' is "Read from sin{n} into sc{n}".
    a text called 'second size' is "Print sc{n}'s size".
    a text called 'close line' is "Close sin{n}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['open line', 'buffer line', 'read line', 'size line', 'empty line', 'second read', 'second line', 'second size', 'close line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Real file I/O, into the program's own per-run scratch directory.

 Plan 324 Part B wanted this behind a path allowlist. It turns out not
 to need one: the program is TOLD its directory on the command line and
 is never given another path, so it cannot write anywhere else by
 construction — and the directory is pid-and-seed keyed and swept after
 the run.

 The earlier attempt wrote to "/dev/stdout" instead, to avoid needing a
 sandbox at all, and that corrupted the harness's own captured output:
 the child got an independent file offset, so it wrote at position 0
 while the parent wrote further on, leaving a sparse hole that read back
 as NUL bytes and turned a golden fixture binary. This is the correct
 target that shortcut was avoiding.

 Write, then read back, then check the size — a round trip, so a defect
 in either direction shows up. Verified by hand first: a program given
 --scratch writes 23 bytes and reads back 23.)

To 'gen leaf file round trip' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    If gen_does_file_io is false then,
        'gen leaf print' of indent,
        Return a boolean, false.
    Set gen_file_ios to gen_file_ios add 1.
    Set gen_texts to gen_texts add 1.
    a number called n is gen_texts.
    a number called 'the payload' is 'rng below' of 100000.
    a number called 'the buffer size' is 'rng below' of 60 add 8.
    (The scratch flag's NAME is drawn per program (gen_manifest.vox), so
     the path line interpolates whatever this program called it. It is
     never empty here: the flag is declared exactly when gen_does_file_io
     is true, which is what got this leaf past its own guard.)
    a text called 'path line' is "a text called fp{n} is \"{{{gen_scratch_flag}}}/out{n}\"".
    a text called 'open write' is "open a file for writing called fw{n} at fp{n}".
    a text called 'write line' is "Write \"payload {'the payload'}\\n\" to fw{n}".
    a text called 'close write' is "Close fw{n}".
    a text called 'open read' is "open a file for reading called fr{n} at fp{n}".
    a text called 'buffer line' is "a buffer called fb{n} is {'the buffer size'} bytes in size".
    a text called 'read line' is "Read from fr{n} into fb{n}".
    a text called 'size line' is "Print fb{n}'s size".
    a text called 'readable line' is "Print fr{n}'s readable".
    a text called 'close read' is "Close fr{n}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['path line', 'open write', 'write line', 'close write', 'open read', 'buffer line', 'read line', 'size line', 'readable line', 'close read'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(Seeded input bytes. Shapes chosen for the boundaries a reader trips
 over: nothing at all, less than a buffer, more than a buffer (the
 truncation path), embedded NULs (a C-string reader stops early, a
 length-bounded one does not — the distinction that buffer bugs live
 in), and a long line with no trailing newline.)
(Input bytes, appended into a dynamic buffer one character at a time —
 not written byte by byte into a fixed one, and not rebuilt as TEXT.

 The obvious route, `Set byte N of buf to value` then `Write b to file`,
 is unavailable twice over. Writing a scalar to a file segfaults
 (compiler bug #40, found doing exactly this). And a fixed-size buffer
 starts EMPTY — `is 4096 bytes in size` sets capacity, not length — so
 indexed writes past the used length are out of bounds and silently do
 nothing. Buffer indexing is also 1-based, which cost a wrong bug report
 before it cost anything else.

 A dynamic buffer sidesteps both: `append` grows it, no index needed.
 It also sidesteps a defect this function used to have (D17): rebuilding
 `gen_input` by `Set gen_input to "{gen_input}{c}"` re-allocates the
 WHOLE text on every character, an O(n^2) copy that measured ~5 GB of
 leaked allocations for 100,000 one-byte appends against ~0.4 MB for the
 same appends into one buffer, since a heap-backed variable is released
 only at program exit and the old copy is never reachable again to free.
 With `shape is 2` drawing up to 999 characters, the old form leaked on
 the order of 0.5 MB per call - real, if smaller than 'gen emit's.

 The cost is that embedded NUL bytes are currently unreachable — a NUL
 cannot be spelled inside a text literal — so the input alphabet is
 printable ASCII. That shape is worth having eventually, because a
 C-string reader stopping early where a length-bounded one does not is
 exactly where buffer bugs live; it needs a byte-level write path that
 does not go through #40.

 Each character is appended straight from its source expression - a
 format string for a digit above 35, 'gen digit for value's own text
 otherwise - rather than bound to a local first: buffer `append` takes
 a format-string source directly (LANGUAGE.md "Format Strings
 Everywhere"), so the intermediate text bought nothing but one more
 allocation per character.)

To 'gen build input'.
    clear 'the built input'.
    a number called shape is 'rng below' of 5.
    If shape is 0 then,
        Set gen_input to 'the built input' as text,
        Return a number, 0.
    a number called 'how many' is 'rng below' of 40 add 1.
    If shape is 2 then,
        Set 'how many' to 'rng below' of 800 add 200.
    a number called i is 0.
    While i is less than 'how many',
        a number called pick is 'rng below' of 62,
        If pick is greater than 35 then,
            append "{pick}" to 'the built input',
        Otherwise,
            append "{'gen digit for value' of pick}" to 'the built input'.
        Set i to i add 1.
    If shape is 4 then,
        Set gen_input to 'the built input' as text,
        Return a number, 0.
    append "\n" to 'the built input'.
    Set gen_input to 'the built input' as text.
    Return a number, 0.

To 'gen write input' with a text called path.
    open a file for writing called 'the input file' at path.
    Write gen_input to 'the input file'.
    Close 'the input file'.
